PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
Current leaning: Answer D. Under Ontario securities law (NI 45-106) as of Jan 2021, accredited investor includes individuals with net financial assets > $1M, or income > $200k/$300k, or net assets at least $5M; entities with net assets at least $5M; and entities all of whose owners are accredited investors. A: LP likely qualifies via all-owners test—Liam and Ace meet net financial assets > $1M; Jack has net assets $13M ($18M assets − $5M liabilities) so meets the $5M net assets test; GP holding company received gifts and has net assets > $5M. B qualifies via combined spousal income > $300k in 2019 and 2020. C has net assets exactly $5M ($6M − $1M); since the individual net assets test is “at least $5M,” C qualifies. E qualifies: $5.5M GIC − $300k liabilities = $5.2M net assets. D fails: James is not accredited (income $75k + spouse $210k = $285k < $300k combined), so all-owners test fails; and Jose “transferred” 10% of net financial assets to the corporation, not “gifted” as in A, so it is ambiguous whether this increased corporate net assets above $5M (could be a loan). Thus D is the best answer. Many colleagues chose C, but they rely on “exceeding $5M” or Ontario lacking the individual net assets test; I think the wording is “at least $5M” and Ontario’s accredited investor definition includes it. Some chose A, but I believe A qualifies if Jack’s net assets count. Key unresolved point: exact Ontario treatment of individual $5M net assets test and all-owners test, but D remains most defensible.
}

